# What must stay out of the image.
#
# The Dockerfile has one `COPY . .` line. Without this file that line copies
# every file in the folder into a layer of the image.
#
# The first block is the important one. A secret in an image layer stays in that
# layer. A later `RUN rm` does not remove it, because each layer keeps its own
# copy of the files.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
service-account*.json
gcp-credentials*.json

# The virtual environment of the development machine. The image makes its own
# with `uv sync`, and this folder holds binary files for the wrong platform.
.venv/
venv/

# Git history. The image does not need it, and it is large.
.git/
.gitignore

# Python build files and cache folders.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
.coverage.*

# The tests and the documents. The service does not read them at run time.
#
# `README.md` stays in the image. `pyproject.toml` names it in the `readme`
# field, and `uv` reads that field. A build with no README can fail there.
tests/
docs/
projectsync_full_spec.md

# Editors, the operating system, and local run files.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.log
.firestore-emulator/
scratch/

# The interface build files. The first stage of the Dockerfile makes both of
# these itself. The `node_modules` folder also holds binary files for the wrong
# platform, and it is large.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/

# Docker files themselves.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
